#speculation(title: [Microvascular Contribution to Handgrip Impairment])[
Exploratory correlations in the post-COVID cohort suggest that part of the grip deficit may track endothelial and inflammatory markers rather than muscle bulk alone.

- Intercellular adhesion molecule-1 (ICAM-1) was higher in patients with reduced recovery than in those with normal recovery @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.
- Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) correlated inversely with the fatigue ratio in patients with ME/CFS (r = −0.26, 95% CI −0.48 to −0.01, p = 0.044) @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.
- Anti-S1 IgG titres correlated negatively with mean grip force in women (r = −0.24, 95% CI −0.44 to −0.01, p = 0.040) @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.
- Thrombocyte count was inversely related to muscle recovery (r = −0.43, 95% CI −0.72 to −0.03, p = 0.030) @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.

These are secondary, exploratory analyses across many comparisons with no multiplicity control, in a single cohort, with small subgroups after sex stratification. They are compatible with a microvascular delivery limitation during repeated contraction — the same construct as the perfusion-versus-production distinction discussed in @sec:perfusion-vs-mito-node — but they do not test it.

*Translation gap:* the markers were measured in blood, while the inferred process is at the muscle microcirculation. The link is indirect and unvalidated.

*Certainty: 0.25.* Falsifiable prediction: if microvascular delivery limits repeated grip performance, then near-infrared spectroscopy during the two-session protocol should show slower reoxygenation in patients with poor recovery, and the ICAM-1/VEGF associations should replicate in an independent ME/CFS cohort. Refuted if muscle oxygenation recovery is normal in patients with low recovery ratios.

*Consequence:* If confirmed, handgrip recovery would become an indirect readout of muscle blood-flow limitation — a cheap proxy for a vascular mechanism that currently needs imaging — but at present it is a hypothesis-generating correlation only.
] <spec:handgrip-microvascular>
